              "type": "Annotation",
              "motivation": [
                "tagging",
                "commenting"
              ],
              "body": [
                {
                  "type": "Feature",
                  "properties": {
                    "label": {
                      "en": [
                        "15/06/1805 - Docking in Bluefields"
                      ]
                    },
                    "date": "N/A",
                    "source": "Lloyd's Register of Shipping"
                  },
                  "geometry": {
                    "type": "Point",
                    "coordinates": [
                      -78.0271847572282,
                      18.1679382772072
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "TextualBody",
                  "purpose": "commenting",
                  "language": "en",
                  "format": "text/html",
                  "value": "<p><b>15/06/1805 - Docking in Bluefields</b></p> <p>As 'the Bedford' sailed along the southwest coast of Jamaica, further plantains and yams were bought for the ship in Bluefields.</p>"
                }
              ],
              "target": "https://jptmoore.github.io/bedford/canvas/1#xywh=3263,3060,144,61"
            },
            {
              "id": "https://jptmoore.github.io/bedford/canvas/1/annotation/14",
              "type": "Annotation",
              "motivation": [
                "tagging",
                "commenting"
              ],
              "body": [
                {
                  "type": "Feature",
                  "properties": {
                    "label": {
                      "en": [
                        "16/06-01/07/1805 - Trafficking enslaved people to plantations from Savanna-la-Mar"
                      ]
                    },
                    "date": "N/A",
                    "source": "Lloyd's Register of Shipping"
                  },
                  "geometry": {
                    "type": "Point",
                    "coordinates": [
                      -78.134096370583,
                      18.2077492374917
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "TextualBody",
                  "purpose": "commenting",
                  "language": "en",
                  "format": "text/html",
                  "value": "<p><b>16/06-01/07/1805 - Trafficking enslaved people to plantations from Savanna-la-Mar</b></p> <p>'The Bedford' received a permit from the Custom House to 'land one hundred and seventy five' enslaved people in Savanna-la-mar having sailed from Kingston.</p>"
                }
              ],
              "target": "https://jptmoore.github.io/bedford/canvas/1#xywh=3263,3060,144,61"
            },
            {
              "id": "https://jptmoore.github.io/bedford/canvas/1/annotation/15",
              "type": "Annotation",
              "motivation": [
                "tagging",
                "commenting"
              ],
              "body": [
                {
                  "type": "Feature",
                  "properties": {
                    "label": {
                      "en": [
                        "02-06/07/1805 - 'Bedford' and 'Chance'"
                      ]
                    },
                    "date": "N/A",
                    "source": "Lloyd's Register of Shipping"
                  },
                  "geometry": {
                    "type": "Point",
                    "coordinates": [
                      -77.8467841397152,
                      18.022184311155
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "TextualBody",
                  "purpose": "commenting",
                  "language": "en",
                  "format": "text/html",
                  "value": "<p><b>02-06/07/1805 - 'Bedford' and 'Chance'</b></p> <p>By early July, the Bedford was in Black River in St Elizabeth parish. Captain Lane was offered three caulkers for hire to be sent to Black River from Kingston to repair the ship, making it watertight by sealing any holes. Two enslaved people were said to still be onboard on 2 July 1805 and this was probably a man referred to as 'Chance' and a boy referred to as 'Bedford' after the ship. They were kept onboard as enslaved crew members, with sailors clothes bought for them at the beginning of July. A wage book shows they were not paid for their service onboard. In fact it was said that 'the Man [Chance] will save his Price in Wages' meaning that the money saved in having him take the place of a crew member would be equal to the money made had he been sold into plantation slavery in Jamaica.</p>"
                }
              ],
              "target": "https://jptmoore.github.io/bedford/canvas/1#xywh=3263,3060,144,61"
            },
            {
