                      18.0674885469807
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "TextualBody",
                  "purpose": "commenting",
                  "language": "en",
                  "format": "text/html",
                  "value": "<p><b>The products of enslaved labour</b></p> <p>Before the Bedford left Jamaica, it was loaded with goods to ship back to England. This included produce harvested on plantations by enslaved people which was being shipped on behalf of Jamaican plantation owners. A ship's manifest noted that it was carrying 'one hundred twenty three Hogsheads Sugar, Forty eight Casks and Three Hundred & Seventeen Bags Coffee, Nineteen Tons Logwood and Four Tons Fustick Twelve Casks Castor Oil & one quarter of a Ton Ivory Imported from the coast of Africa'. The majority of the sugar and coffee can be traced to plantations in St Elizabeth parish, of which Black River is the capital, and Manchester, the next parish over to the east. These included plantations and enslaved people that were managed or owned by William Rowe. Ten casks of the coffee shipped came from Henry Palmer's coffee plantations in Manchester, including Greenland. By 1817, 127 individuals were enslaved at these plantations whose bonded labour cultivated the coffee.</p>"
                }
              ],
              "target": "https://jptmoore.github.io/bedford/canvas/1#xywh=3263,3060,144,61"
